                <head>350. Editorial Note</head>
                <p>President Eisenhower visited Turkey
                    December 6–7, during his 11-nation good will tour (December 3–23). The President
                    and his party arrived at Ankara from Italy at 3 p.m. on December 6. After an
                    airport greeting from President Bayar and
                    ceremonial functions, including a wreath-laying at the tomb of Ataturk, the
                    President met with Bayar, Prime Minister
                        Menderes, and Foreign Minister
                        Zorlu at the Presidential Palace.
                    (See <ref target="#d351">Document 351</ref>) After this meeting, the President
                    attended a formal dinner given by Bayar at
                    8:30 p.m. He returned to his residence at 11 p.m. At 7:30 a.m. on December 7,
                    the President, accompanied by President Bayar, left by helicopter for Esenboga Airport. He left Ankara
                    for Karachi at 8:10 a.m., following brief airport ceremonies. For text of the
                    joint communiqué issued after Eisenhower’s talks with
                    Turkish leaders, see Department of State <hi rendition="#italic">Bulletin</hi>,
                    December 28, 1959, pages 932–933. Eisenhower’s
                    recollections of the meeting are in <hi rendition="#italic">Waging Peace</hi>,
                    pages 492–493.</p>
            </div>
